**Weaknesses:**

1. **Real-world datasets may not suffer from genuine oversquashing, making empirical validation of the proposed method inconclusive.** The paper evaluates on TUDataset benchmarks (ENZYMES, IMDB-B, MUTAG, NCII, PROTEINS), which are relatively small graph classification tasks. As noted in reviews of related curvature-based rewiring work: *"It is unclear whether any dataset in Table 1 would possess bottlenecks that hinder (in particular long-range) interactions that might be necessary for the task"* and *"do any of these datasets actually suffer from oversquashing?"* (EcrdmRT99M, LkB80Rw6Ap). The observed performance gains could be artifacts of added connections rather than evidence that oversquashing was mitigated. Consideration of long-range benchmarks (e.g., LRGB) would strengthen the empirical claims. — Inspired by /u/wg25r/review_agent/human_reviews/EcrdmRT99M.md (Reviewer 1, W1) and /u/wg25r/review_agent/human_reviews/LkB80Rw6Ap.md (Reviewer 3, W3).

2. **Inequity or absence of hyperparameter tuning undermines attributing performance gains to the method.** The paper explicitly states it uses *"fixed, dataset- and model-agnostic hyperparameters"* for rewiring, diverging from prior work that performs hyperparameter sweeps. This makes it impossible to know whether improvements come from the rewiring strategy itself or from the specific hyperparameter setting. Related work on rewiring has been criticized because *"state-of-the-art results from curvature-based rewiring often stem from hyperparameter tuning rather than consistent performance improvements"* (EcrdmRT99M), and other rewiring papers have been flagged for *"hyperparameter tuning... not performed for the comparison methods"* (4Ua4hKiAJX, Reviewer 2c). — Inspired by /u/wg25r/review_agent/human_reviews/EcrdmRT99M.md (Reviewers 1, 4) and /u/wg25r/review_agent/human_reviews/4Ua4hKiAJX.md (Reviewer 2).

3. **Incremental theoretical novelty—the main results are direct extensions of existing GNN bounds.** Lemma 3.2 extends the Jacobian sensitivity bound from GNNs to relational structures; Proposition 3.4 mirrors Fesser & Weber (2023, Proposition 3.4); Theorem 3.5 follows a nearly identical proof structure to Di Giovanni et al. (2023, Theorem 4.1). While the unifying framework itself has value, reviewers of similar work have noted that *"the theoretical results, while relevant, seem to follow directly from prior works. It is not clear whether the contribution is significant theoretically, as opposed to being a merger of known prior results"* and *"the architectural changes are incremental"* and *"the theoretical contributions feel somewhat straightforward, offering limited new insights"* (NmcOAwRyH5, Ax3uliEBVR). The paper should more clearly articulate what new analytical insight is gained beyond restating existing bounds in new notation. — Inspired by /u/wg25r/review_agent/human_reviews/NmcOAwRyH5.md (Reviewer 4) and /u/wg25r/review_agent/human_reviews/Ax3uliEBVR.md (Reviewers 2, 3).

